<o:Entity Id="o158">
<a:ObjectID>156D779F-AEEC-4C4D-9389-4DD05F42D893</a:ObjectID>
<a:Name>CUSTOMER</a:Name>
<a:Code>CSTMR</a:Code>
<a:CreationDate>1726127944</a:CreationDate>
<a:Creator>Koyel.Ghatak</a:Creator>
<a:ModificationDate>1726145022</a:ModificationDate>
<a:Modifier>Koyel.Ghatak</a:Modifier>
<a:Comment>The role of the Party that takes title to purchased goods or services purchased from Shell. It includes prospect customers too.  In Shell context a B2B Customer is reserved for business organizations or persons, including retail stores, but excluding end consumers (e.g.people buying petrol/gasoline to put in their vehicle) that purchase a product for their own use in the pursuit of its business. Customers may use the products for a variety of reasons (e.g. manufacturing, transport, heating, etc) or on-sell them to others and are invoiced directly by Shell for goods and services. Customers can also include intermediaries such as dealers and distributors who on-sell to consumers or other intermediaries. Although a customer is typically an organisation which buys from Shell, the seller in that transaction can also be a Shell company (eg Shell Italy can sell a product to Shell France, and that transaction can incur a tax liability).Technically a Customer is denoted based on a 1-1 relationship with a CUSTOMER_SOLD_TO entity, which represents the Account and interaction with the Shell sales organisation.

We can map it to -SAP table: KNA1
</a:Comment>
